Email View Design (in the same spirit as the Note View)

The Email View follows the same clean, Apple-like philosophy: minimal, calm, elegant, adaptive, with complexity hidden behind simple taps.

### Background system contract

The inbox surface is fed by AFP plus background daemons, not by direct mailbox UI mutations.

- `Email Gateway Daemon` ingests real mail from IMAP/SMTP or provider APIs, converts it into AFP, and sends outbound AFP email intents as real email.
- `Relational Sync Worker` maintains fast mailbox-style Postgres projections for folder/thread views when AFP messages opt into `store_in_relational: true`.
- `Archive & Cold Storage Daemon` can move older AFP-backed email history to MinIO cold storage while leaving lightweight indexes in Postgres so archived threads remain discoverable.
- `Health & Maintenance Daemon` improves labels, duplicate detection, embeddings, and graph hygiene during idle windows.

Design implication:

- The Email View should always render from the best available derived representation, but it must fall back cleanly to AFP message content when a daemon-produced projection is incomplete, rebuilding, or unavailable.
- All of this remains under the Master Agent, with delegated scoped keys, asynchronous subscriptions, E2EE preservation, and append-only history.

**Layout (same structure as Note View for consistency)**

- **Top Bar** (56px)  
  Search bar (full-text + semantic across inbox)  
  New Email button (primary action, uses sunset orange accent)  
  View toggle: Inbox / Sent / Drafts / Archive

- **Left Sidebar** (240px, collapsible)  
  Folders: Inbox, Sent, Drafts, Archive, Trash  
  Smart Folders (AI-created, e.g. “Travel”, “Finance”, “Waiting for Reply”)  
  Labels / Supertags (from Knowledge Curator)

- **Main Content Area**  
  Email list as clean cards:  
  - Sender avatar + name  
  - Subject line (bold if unread)  
  - First line of body or AI-generated summary  
  - Supertags as small colored pills  
  - Timestamp on the right  
  Hover: subtle lift + quick actions (Archive, Reply, Star)

Archive behavior:
- Archive is a user-facing mailbox state first.
- Older history may also be cold-stored by the archive daemon behind the scenes, but the UI should preserve lightweight discoverability and avoid exposing storage internals to the user.

- **Right Sidebar** (preview / composer)  
  When an email is selected: full readable view with backlinks to related notes/research.  
  When composing: the composer replaces the preview area (see question 4 below for flow).

All interactions feel instant and calm — same spacing, typography, and transitions as the Note View.

### 4. Composer Design: Popup vs Full View Replacement vs Shadow

**Full view replacement (remove inbox, render composer)** is **noticeably slower and more jarring** for the user:
- After sending, the system must re-render the entire inbox list → visible flicker and delay.
- User loses context (they can no longer see the original email thread while writing).

**Better design: Shadowed overlay with side-drawer composer**

- Tap **New Email** → inbox gets a subtle dark overlay (shadow) and a **side-drawer composer** slides in from the right (340px wide, same as right sidebar).
- The original inbox stays visible in the background (dimmed).
- User can still scroll the inbox while writing if needed.
- After sending, the drawer closes smoothly, the shadow fades, and the inbox returns to full brightness with the new email highlighted.
- This feels fast, contextual, and Apple-like (similar to how macOS Mail or iOS Mail handles compose).

This is the recommended UX: minimal disruption, maximum context, and no full re-render of the inbox.
